      "dom_id": "question-45601",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The area of an oil patch, measured in square kilometers, is given by the function $A(t)=10\\cdot (1.1)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in days since the pollution occurred. How many square kilometers does the area increase by between day $1$ and day $2?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the growth of the patch between day $1$ and day $2$, we need to subtract the area after day $1$ from the area after day $2.$ So we need to calculate\n\n$$\nA(2)-A(1).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the area after the first day, $A(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(1) & =10\\cdot (1.1)^{1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1.1 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $A(2),$ the area after the second day:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2) & =10\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1.21 \\\\  & =12.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth of the patch between day $1$ and day $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2)-A(1) & =12.1-11=1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area increased by $1.1$ square kilometer between day $1$ and day $2.$",
