      "dom_id": "question-45697",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The population $M(t)$ of a colony of rodents is given by $M(t)=1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in years since the beginning of the observations. How many additional rodents will be born in the colony between years $2$ and $3?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the growth in the population of rodents between year $2$ and year $3$, we need to subtract the population after year $2$ from the population after year $3.$ So we need to calculate\n\n$$\nM(3)-M(2).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the population after the second year, $M(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(2) & =1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{(2)} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\cdot 2.56 \\\\  & =2\\,560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $M(3),$ the population after the third year:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(3) & =1\\,000\\cdot (1.6)^{(3)} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\cdot 4.096 \\\\  & =4\\,096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the population between year $2$ and year $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(3)-M(2) & =4\\,096-2\\,560=1\\,536.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the population of rodents increased by $1\\,536$ between years $2$ and $3.$",
